For a cubic \(P(x)=k x^{3}+p x^{2}+q x+r\) a local extremum at \(x=x_{0}\) occurs when  

\[
P'(x_{0})=0 .
\]

Hence for the three polynomials we have  

\[
\begin{aligned}
A'(x) &= 3a x^{2}+2abx-4, &\qquad A'(b)=0 &\Longrightarrow 5ab^{2}=4,\\[2pt]
B'(x) &= 3b x^{2}+2bcx-6, &\qquad B'(c)=0 &\Longrightarrow 5bc^{2}=6,\\[2pt]
C'(x) &= 3c x^{2}+2ca x-9, &\qquad C'(a)=0 &\Longrightarrow 5ca^{2}=9.
\end{aligned}
\]

---

### 1.  Express \(a\) and \(b\) in terms of \(c\)

From the first relation  

\[
a=\frac{4}{5b^{2}}. \tag{1}
\]

From the second relation  

\[
b=\frac{6}{5c^{2}}. \tag{2}
\]

Insert (2) into (1):

\[
a=\frac{4}{5\left(\dfrac{6}{5c^{2}}\right)^{2}}
   =\frac{4}{5\cdot\frac{36}{25c^{4}}}
   =\frac{4}{\frac{180}{25c^{4}}}
   =\frac{100c^{4}}{180}
   =\frac{5c^{4}}{9}. \tag{3}
\]

---

### 2.  Solve for \(c\)

Use the third relation \(5ca^{2}=9\) with \(a\) from (3):

\[
5c\left(\frac{5c^{4}}{9}\right)^{2}=9
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
5c\frac{25c^{8}}{81}=9
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\frac{125c^{9}}{81}=9.
\]

Multiply by \(81\):

\[
125c^{9}=729=3^{6}.
\]

Thus  

\[
c^{9}=\frac{3^{6}}{5^{3}}
\Longrightarrow
c=\left(\frac{3^{6}}{5^{3}}\right)^{1/9}
   =\frac{3^{2/3}}{5^{1/3}}
   =\left(\frac{9}{5}\right)^{1/3}. \tag{4}
\]

---

### 3.  Find \(b\) and \(a\)

Using (2) and (4):

\[
b=\frac{6}{5c^{2}}
   =\frac{6}{5\left(\dfrac{9}{5}\right)^{2/3}}
   =\frac{6}{5^{1/3}\,9^{2/3}}. \tag{5}
\]

Using (3) and (4):

\[
a=\frac{5c^{4}}{9}
   =\frac{5}{9}\left(\dfrac{9}{5}\right)^{4/3}
   =\frac{9^{1/3}}{5^{1/3}}
   =\left(\frac{9}{5}\right)^{1/3}. \tag{6}
\]

Notice that \(a=c\).

---

### 4.  Compute \(abc\)

\[
\begin{aligned}
abc &= a\,b\,c
     = c^{2}\,b \\
    &= \left(\frac{9}{5}\right)^{2/3}
       \frac{6}{5^{1/3}\,9^{2/3}} \\[4pt]
    &= 6\;\frac{9^{2/3}}{5^{2/3}}
       \frac{1}{5^{1/3}9^{2/3}}
     = 6\;\frac{1}{5}
     = \frac{6}{5}.
\end{aligned}
\]

---

\[
\boxed{\dfrac{6}{5}}
\]

